      "writtenOn": "2021-11-09T13:34:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Right. I had that in the back of my mind, too. The old byte code interpreter used GL-style naming to reflect all of the uniforms (where names were emitted for each individual slot - iterating over arrays and fields, etc.). So you could learn that the 57th slot was actually \"myUniformArray[3].x\". That ends up being REALLY chatty, and generates lots of long string names for things, which may or may not be a great experience. The current code uses UniformInfo to reflect things at the top level about each declared uniform (but not individual elements inside) - so there\u0027s a type enum, slot count, array size, and that\u0027s about it (plus the name).",
      "parentUuid": "a014f452_609141b8",
      "range": {
